area is 2√3+√11 = √12+√11. Hence m+n = 23.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_10B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"id":"rhombus-diagonals-and-altitudes","label":"Rhombus diagonals and altitudes","nodeIds":["cnm_189","cnm_174","cnm_137","cnm_p148"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_169"],"id":"solution:rhombus-diagonals-and-altitudes:0824ce71efe8","label":"Rhombus diagonals and triangle heights","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rhombus-diagonals-and-altitudes":["cnm_p150","cnm_190","cnm_189","cnm_137"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_190","cnm_189","cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 23","reason":"The equilateral-triangle pairs give AC = AD = 2√3 and areas [ABC] = [ADE] = √3. Triangle ACD has base 2 and altitude √((2√3)² - 1²) = √11, so its area is √11. Thus the pentagon's area is 2√3 + √11 = √12 + √11, giving m + n = 23.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12B_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"rhombus-diagonals-and-altitudes","label":"Rhombus diagonals and altitudes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rhombus-diagonals-and-altitudes":["cnm_189","cnm_174","cnm_137","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_189","cnm_174","cnm_137","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 20","reason":"AD=AP+PD=5, and the rhombus gives AB=5. Since BP is perpendicular to AD, BP=√(5²-3²)=4. Therefore the area is AD×BP=5×4=20.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12B_Q02","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_189","cnm_p105","cnm_075"],"id":"method_1","label":"Base and perpendicular height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"method_1":["cnm_p147","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds